<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">1</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L14" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">Explain what is meant by a convex optimization problem and how to determine if an optimization problem is convex. You can use Exercise 1 connected to the lecture \342\200\235Convex optimization\342\200\235 as a guide for your explanation</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L16" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L99" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">A convex optimization problem is a problem where the cost function is convex over a convex feasible set. This implies that the feasible set/region we are working within is also convex. Thereby any minimizer of f is also convex, and any local minimum must also be the global minimum.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L17" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L104" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Convexity in general is determined by the possibility of drawing a straight line from one point to another, without having any values along the line which are not within the set:
<Image height="338" width="412" zoomable="false" labelreference="L7" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjJCIiISEiIi0lKUJPVU5EU19ZR0YnLSUtQk9VTkRTX1dJRFRIRzYjJCIlU1RGKi0lLkJPVU5EU19IRUlHSFRHNiMkIiUrTUYqLSUpQ0hJTERSRU5HNiI=</Drawing-Root></Image><Image height="373" width="359" zoomable="false" labelreference="L9" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjJCIlP1QhIiItJSlCT1VORFNfWUc2IyQiIiFGKi0lLUJPVU5EU19XSURUSEc2IyQiJTVPRiotJS5CT1VORFNfSEVJR0hURzYjJCIlXVBGKi0lKUNISUxEUkVORzYi</Drawing-Root></Image><Image height="355" width="198" zoomable="false" labelreference="L11" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjJCIlNXghIiItJSlCT1VORFNfWUc2IyQiIiFGKi0lLUJPVU5EU19XSURUSEc2IyQiJSs/RiotJS5CT1VORFNfSEVJR0hURzYjJCIlcU5GKi0lKUNISUxEUkVORzYi</Drawing-Root></Image><Image height="352" width="245" zoomable="false" labelreference="L13" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjJCImSSsiISIiLSUpQk9VTkRTX1lHNiMkIiQ/JkYqLSUtQk9VTkRTX1dJRFRIRzYjJCIlcUNGKi0lLkJPVU5EU19IRUlHSFRHNiMkIiVTTkYqLSUpQ0hJTERSRU5HNiI=</Drawing-Root></Image></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L15" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">It is important to note that while a subset might be convex, a larger subset might not, as can be seen in figure 2.5 and 2.6. However, each of the nonconvex sets can be split into smaller convex sets.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L118" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When creating a region for a set, one can either create a region going strictly between points, or create a hull, which is convex.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L119" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L121" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When writing a convex cost function, R is often denoted with a subscript c, to imply convexity:
<Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L122" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">A set R_c is convex if any two points and the line segment between them lies entirely within the set, as can also be seen in the figures. Mathematically speaking it means that a set R_c C R^n is convex is any two points x_1, x_2\342\210\210R_c for any alpha\342\210\210[0,1]. the last part in laymans terms means that if point x_1 is at 0 and point x_2 is at 1, then alpha is a parameter which denotes where along the line the convex combination is.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L123" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L124" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When determining whether or not an optimization problem is convex or not, the feasible set and the objective functions convexity each has to be checked. The feasible set is convex if it fits within linear constraints and otherwise meet the aforementioned. Convex sets include spaces in n dimensions, halvspaces, affine subspaces, convex polytopes, etc. The objective functions convexity is found either by the inequality:
<Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L125" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">or by being twice continuosly differentiable with a Hessian matrix being positive semidefinite everywhere in the feasible region. Some convex functions are: linear functions, quadratic functions with PSD matrix, norms, negative logarithms, exponentials, hinge loss, ReLU etc.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
