<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L168" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Key Uses of Convexity:
Guarantees Only One Best Solution
<Font encoding="UTF-8">In a convex problem, if you find a solution that seems best (called a minimum), you can be sure it's the best possible\342\200\224there are no \342\200\234trap\342\200\235 points or fake minimums. This is like hiking in a valley that has only one lowest point: you won\342\200\231t get stuck on a \342\200\234false bottom.\342\200\235
</Font>
Makes Problems Easier to Solve
<Font encoding="UTF-8">When a problem is convex, computers and algorithms can solve it much faster and more reliably. It\342\200\231s like having a smooth, straight path to your goal, instead of a mountain trail with lots of peaks and valleys.
</Font>
Efficient Algorithms
<Font encoding="UTF-8">Because convex problems behave predictably, we have very efficient tools (like gradient descent) to solve them\342\200\224no need to try every possible solution by brute force.
</Font>
Wide Applications
Convexity is used everywhere:

Engineering: Designing safest, cheapest structures

Machine Learning: Training AI to make better predictions

Finance: Finding the best way to invest money

Logistics: Planning delivery routes or schedules

Data Fitting: Drawing the best line through a cloud of data points</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
