<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L220" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When applying both methods to the co2 data, the steepest descent algorithm fails, as the condition number is high, meaning that the contours of the surface are highly elongated, effectively meaning that only a small descent is present. This leads to an inefficient zig-zaggin slow progress towards the minimum. To ensure efficient step sizes in the &quot;steep&quot; direction, the sizes should be chosen somewhere between &quot;too tiny to ever reach minima&quot; and &quot;so large that overshoots and divergence will result in eternal oscillation&quot;</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L221" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L222" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">However, the newton-raphson succeeds because of the second derivative, which takes into account the curvature, effectively rescaling the space and directly moving towards the minimum. For quadratic cost functions such as this, newtons method converges in one step from any point, as long as there are no regularization/constraints.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
