<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L37" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Linear regression is a classic parametric method. The model assumes a specific fixed form and has a finite number of parameters w, regardless of how much data is presented. The model form is: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> or more generally <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> where wi are fixed parameters and phii(x) are chosen basis functions. Parameters are typically estimated using techniques such as maximum likelihood estimation, maximum a posteriori or least squares. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L397" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L398" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">A nonparametric method could be the k-nearest neighbours (KNN). This model effectively stores the entire training dataset, meaning that as datasize increases, the memory and computation requirements also increases. The model works by predicting output of a new datapoint, by looking at the k closest points in the training data and average their outputs. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L35" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L399" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Parametric Methods
Fixed number of parameters: The number of parameters is independent of dataset size.
Assume a specific functional form: e.g., linear, Gaussian, etc.
Estimation: Learn the parameters of this fixed-form model from data.
Efficient for small to medium datasets: Once trained, prediction is fast.
Limitation: If the assumed model is wrong, performance may be poor (lack of flexibility).

Nonparametric Methods
Model complexity grows with data: More data = higher complexity (e.g., k-NN stores all data).
Fewer assumptions about the functional form: More flexible, can adapt to more complex patterns.
Estimation: May require storing all or much of the data; often need more data for good performance.
Flexible, can approximate more complicated functions: But can be slower and more memory-intensive.
Limitation: Prediction can be slower (since all data might need to be searched), and overfitting is possible if not regularized.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
