<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L413" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Looking at the confusion matrices, it becomes apparent that while the KNN method can be better than the gaussian and kernel naive bayes, then it required several iterations to find the optimal number. For knn=10 the predictability is 92%, but for knn=3 it is 95, which is loads better than than both gaussian naive bayes implementations, rated at 90 and 91% respectively. It seems that no matter which value is chosen for the kernel naive bayes, the result cannot get better than approx 91%</Text-field>
</Input>
