<Section collapsed="true" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">6</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L39" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">Explain the general model of linear regression with different possible regularizations. You can use Exercise 1 connected to the lecture \342\200\235Linear Regression\342\200\235 as a guide to your explanation.
</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L41" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">in linear regression the desire is to make a straight line which is as close as possible to all point within a dataset, i.e. a scatter plot. The line itself is then capable of predicting an y dependent on an x value, or the other way around. The main idea is ideally a least squares solution, where the sum of all distances from the line to point in the plot, is as small as possible. Mathematically this can be written as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. One way to achieve the smallest possible squared sum would be to create a high-degree polynomial intersecting all points. However, this is far from linear, and will almost never work for predicting future values. Think of sampling rates and sinusoids, and their relation. This is called overfitting, and will despite fitting sample data perfectly, make the model too complex, essentially measuring noise instead of patterns.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L435" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L436" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Regularization comes into play to prevent overfitting by penalizing the model for being too complex. There exist several types of regularization; ridge, lasso and elastic net. Ridge regularization penalizes the regression by adding up all the squares of the weights. Intuitively this should result in the model finding a sweetspot where no weight is too large. Lasso regularization functions similarly by adding up the absolute values of the weights. Intuitively this should yield some weight to be exactly zero, and most others to be close to zero. The elastic net is a combination of both.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L433" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
