<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L47" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Binary logistic regression is the idea of classifying the entire dataset into two features. Multinomial logistic regression is the idea of classifiyng the entire dataset into k features.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L443" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L444" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The binary logistic regression functions by using the sigmoid:
<Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L439" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">which is the sigmoid curve at point a along the x-axis.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L445" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Image height="260" width="339" zoomable="false" labelreference="L45" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjJCIjNSEiIi0lKUJPVU5EU19ZRzYjJCIiIUYqLSUtQk9VTkRTX1dJRFRIRzYjJCIlNU1GKi0lLkJPVU5EU19IRUlHSFRHNiMkIiU/RUYqLSUpQ0hJTERSRU5HNiI=</Drawing-Root></Image></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L440" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The sigmoid function works by taking a value a (often along the lines of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, and then computing a value above or below 0.5. If the value is below, the value is classified as 0, and if the value is above, it is classified as 1. The further from 0.5, more confidence is made for the classification.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L441" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L448" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The likelihood is given by:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L451" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L449" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L452" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">which is the binary cross-entropy (negative log-likelihood). To estimate the parameters a gradient descent or variants thereof is usually used, since the loss is convex in w but does not have a closed form solution.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L463" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L464" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Multinomial logistic regression is used when k different classes is present. The purpose is predicting the propability that an input x belongs to each class, and the feature vector is given by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and output by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. For each class, the linear score is given by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, where wk is the weight vector for class k. With that in mind, the softmax function can be introduced as:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L465" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L466" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Where the denominator ensures the probabilities sum to 1 across all k classes. In matrix notation, a dataset of N samples is given by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, where <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L469" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">With a given dataset <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the loss to minimize at the negative log-likelihood is:
</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L467" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>                                                   <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> means probability of the target y equals class k given input x</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-output="false" inline-output="false" labelreference="L470" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L471" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Then, when it comes to deciding which class a new input x should be put in, the highest propability is found by: <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L457" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Lastly, note that a key insight is that if naive bayes assumptions hold, logistic regression weights correspond to log-odds ratios from naive bayes.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L472" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L473" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L458" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When selecting which features to include in the model, they should be chosen with caution. Including only relevant features will improve generalization, as relevant features reduces overfitting. Moreover, too many irrelevant/noisy features increase the variance of the estimated coefficients, harming predictive performance. Even more important is the fact, that the fewer amount of parameters/features results in faster computation, as simpler models can be made. However, removing too many features could result in missing important information.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L453" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L459" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Removing features can be done by ridge or lasso regularization, as both can help drive certain coefficients towards zero, removing them from the model. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L460" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L454" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">When performing the exercise, we can see an increase in true and false positives, and a decrease in both negatives, which is good in the sense of cancer, but in reality the total number of false readings is higher, which is bad as a whole. If it  positive meant which targets to bomb, the added number of targets which maybe shouldn't have been bombed is bad, so in that respect, more false negatives is a better alternative. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L455" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Image height="508" width="600" zoomable="false" labelreference="L47" drawcaption="false" captionalignment="0" captionposition="1" bookmark=""><Drawing-Root>LSUrQU5OT1RBVElPTkc2Jy0lKUJPVU5EU19YRzYjLCQtJSZGbG9hdEc2IyUpaW5maW5pdHlHISIiLSUpQk9VTkRTX1lHRictJS1CT1VORFNfV0lEVEhHRictJS5CT1VORFNfSEVJR0hUR0YnLSUpQ0hJTERSRU5HNiI=</Drawing-Root></Image></Text-field>
