<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L483" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">With a single perceptron established, the multilayer perceptron can be introduced. The MLP comprises an input layer, a number of hidden layers, and an output layer. A single layer perceptron can only separate data with a straight line/plane, while a MLP can do nonlinear problems. A single hidden layer can theoretically result in a complete system handling all possibilities, but would be more computationally demanding than a model n layers deep. Basically the input layers takes the raw features, such as pixels in an image. The hidden layers receives values from the previous layer, does computations and passes it to the next layer. The output layer produces the final result, whether it is classification, regression or something else. It is extremely important to twist inputs between neutrons, as they otherwise would not gain the complete knowledge of previous layers. In total, a deeper network can achieve the same computations with far fewer neurons than a single layer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L478" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L484" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">But how do MLP's learn? It starts with random weights, making wild guesses, but with training examples it adjusts the weights incrementally to minimize erros, until convergence is reached. the loss function is for regression using MLPs is mean squared error:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
